<main><section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Physics · Time Series · ML</div><h1>ATLAS TileCal Predictive Maintenance</h1><p class="section-lead">Using machine-learning methods to classify and predict detector alert signals in a high-stakes scientific operating environment.</p></div></section><section><div class="container content"><h2>Context</h2><p>Detector monitoring produces operational signals that need to be understood in context. The modelling challenge is to distinguish useful patterns from noise while preserving the physical meaning of the system being monitored.</p><h2>Approach</h2><ul><li>Frame alert signals as a classification and prediction problem.</li><li>Use time-series structure rather than treating observations as exchangeable rows.</li><li>Connect model behaviour back to detector operations and physics context.</li><li>Evaluate whether a prediction could support earlier, better-informed intervention.</li></ul><div class="callout">This work sits at the boundary between scientific research and applied predictive maintenance: the model only matters if its output remains meaningful in the operating context.</div><h2>What this case proves</h2><p>I can work with technically complex domain data, translate scientific context into a machine-learning formulation, and evaluate predictive systems with the operational decision in mind.</p><p><a href="/case-studies">← Back to case studies</a></p></div></section></main>
